fact WorkItem(id: "WI-975", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-956 item 4. Not fixed there: this is a LANGUAGE decision, and WI-956's gate fix is right whichever way it goes.\n\nWHAT LOADS TODAY, clean, no diagnostic:\n\n    namespace n\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)     -- §6.3 sugar: desugars to `sort Rec { entity Rec(n: Int64) }`\n      sort Rec                 -- the SAME name, declared again, with a different body\n        sort T = ?\n        operation peek(x: T) -> T\n      end\n    end\n\n`Rec` ends up `kinds == [Entity, Sort]` with `type_params_of_sort(Rec) == [\"T\"]` and a member `Rec.peek`. Nothing warns that `Rec` was declared twice.\n\nAND IT IS HALF-WIRED. The second declaration's body does not resolve names from its enclosing namespace. MEASURED, three separate shapes:\n  - `operation peek(r: Rec) -> T`         -> \"unresolved name 'Rec' in scope 'peek'\"\n  - `provides Resource[C = Counter, ...]` -> \"unresolved name 'Resource' in scope 'Counter'\", and the same for `Counter` and for the IMPORTED `Int64`\nOnly a body referring exclusively to names declared INSIDE it (like `peek(x: T) -> T` above) loads. So the shape is accepted, but a sort declared this way can carry no `provides`, no member typed by its own sort, and no imported type.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE ODDITY. This is the shape that made WI-956's `kind_of` bug reachable: `kind_of` reports the FIRST-declared category, so a sort registered `[Entity, Sort]` failed five `kind is Sort` gates and `call_bracket_scopes(Rec.peek)` came back EMPTY. WI-956 fixed the gates (they were asking a display question at a membership site, wrong regardless). The declaration itself is untouched.\n\nTHE DECISION, which the spec should make, not a reader:\n  §6.3 says the sugar and the long form are an EQUIVALENCE -- `entity Account(...)` and `sort Account { entity Account(...) }` denote the same thing. It does not say whether a LATER `sort Account { ... }` EXTENDS that sort, CONFLICTS with it, or is simply undefined behaviour. Pick one:\n  (a) REFUSE -- a duplicate sort declaration is a load error naming both spans. Simplest, and consistent with \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\".\n  (b) MERGE -- define re-declaration as extension, and then the scope wiring is a real bug to fix (the second body must see its namespace).\n  (c) Something else the spec author intends.\n\nDo NOT pick (b) by default just because it currently half-works.\n\nACCEPTANCE: docs/kernel-language.md §6.3 states the rule; the loader implements it; a test drives the fixture above and pins whichever answer was chosen (if (a), pin the diagnostic text and the two spans; if (b), pin that `provides` and an imported type resolve inside the second body). cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: WI-956 item 4 (see wi956_kind_gate_tests in typing.rs, whose fixture is this shape)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-08-08T13:21:16Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-975", author: "user", content: "CLOSED 2026-08-08. Resolved by WI-997: the pass-1 declaration ledger now refuses two written type declarations sharing one scope and local name, with diagnostics and controls for the legal eponymous-constructor shape. Proposal 059 keeps that decision as R1.", at: "2026-08-08T13:27:08Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-975", author: "user", content: "059 FINAL REVIEW (2026-08-04) — four independent reviewers: internal consistency,\nclaims-vs-implementation, claims-vs-cited-sources, completeness-vs-grammar.\n\nVERDICT: 059 IS NOT YET DEFINED. An implementer cannot build it without asking\nquestions the document does not answer. Seven blocking design calls, five\nmisrepresentations of cited sources, and R3's exhaustiveness claim is false.\n\nDeduped and ranked below. Three factual errors were fixed in place immediately\n(listed last); everything else needs a human verdict and is left standing.\n\n=== BLOCKING — the document does not decide these ===\n\nB1. THE RULE BAN HAS A HOLE THROUGH A CONSTRUCT R3 ALLOWS. `_provides_content`\n(grammar.js:961) admits rule_declaration, rule_block, fact_declaration and\nproof_declaration, and load.rs:16554 loads them through the ordinary `load_rule`\npath. So `provides Show[T=Rec] language anthill rule show(?r) = \"x\" end` inside a\nsecondary entry reaches EVERY hazard R3's ban is justified by -- non-monotone\nsearch, and the `[simp]`-rewrites-in-the-typer one, since `[simp]` is read off\nkb.rule_meta and a rule is exactly its carrier. R3's \"exhaustive over an entry's\nDIRECT content\" puts the block's interior out of the lists' reach and gives it no\nverdict -- unlike the nested namespace and the nested sort, which each get an\nexplicit non-recursion verdict with a principled reason. DECIDE: do R3's refusals\nrecurse into a provides block? If not, R3's central refusal is decorative.\n\nB2. THE CLASSIFICATION PREDICATE IS ORDER-DEPENDENT, AND DISAGREES WITH THE\nDEFINITIONS. Two defects in one line (059:60, `has_kind(X, Sort)`).\n  (a) MEASURED: `namespace Rec {…}` written BEFORE `sort Rec {…}` leaves kinds\n      [\"Namespace\"] -- load.rs:2700 reuses a symbol found by qualified name and\n      never calls add_kind(Sort) on the reuse arm. Contrast Item::Entity\n      (load.rs:2864), which records roles FROM THE DECLARATION, which is why the\n      free-standing-entity spelling is order-insensitive and this one is not. So\n      the predicate 059 offers as \"the question an implementation asks\" answers\n      FALSE for a genuine secondary entry in one of the two orders, and any\n      R3/R4 gate keyed on it fails OPEN precisely where WI-979 already breaks.\n      059 flags the symptom at line 64 but states the predicate unconditionally;\n      the two belong in one sentence.\n  (b) `enum X`, `sort X = T` and `sort X = ?` are all Sort-kind, so has_kind\n      classifies a namespace beside any of them as a secondary entry -- while the\n      Definitions admit only \"`sort X … end` or `entity X(…)`\" as a main entry.\n      The verdict decides whether R3 refuses that namespace's rules and facts.\n\nB3. `import_clause` IS UNCLASSIFIED AND CONTRADICTORY, AND AN IMPORT CAPTURE\nREACHES NO R4 CLAUSE. It is in _namespace_content and in neither R3 list, so\nline 72's \"anything unlisted is refused pending classification\" refuses it --\ndirectly against line 90, which measures imports working and calls the sharing\n\"deliberate rather than tolerated\". Worse, line 90 states \"an import is a CAPTURE\nROUTE exactly as a declaration is\" and then draws the wrong conclusion: all three\nR4 clauses key on the CAPTURING side being a member declaration, and an import\ndeclares no member. `namespace Rec { import other.f }` shadowing a bare `f` that\nan existing main-entry body reads is line 162's identical measured hazard,\nadmitted by every clause.\n\nB4. \"ENTRY\" IS NEVER INDIVIDUATED, AND THREE RULES KEY ON IT. R3's proof clause\n(\"declared in another entry\"), the narrow rule's condition (2) (\"every clause of\nthat head is written in that same entry\"), and R4 clause 2 all turn on entry\nidentity. Line 123 says (2) is decidable \"by grouping a predicate's clauses by\nthe entry they are written in\" and never gives the key. One `namespace X … end`\nblock? All text at that address in one file? A file? Line 117's \"two secondary\nentries that each introduce freshp\" implies per-block; nothing states it.\n\nB5. \"PROVISION\" IS NEVER DEFINED, BUT R3'S FACT RULE IS A PARTITION OF FACTS.\n\"allow `provides`/`fact Spec[X]`, refuse every other fact\" needs a loader\npredicate `is_provision(fact)`. There is no `Spec` symbol kind (intern.rs:30) and\nno `spec` keyword -- a spec is an ordinary sort -- so the discriminator has to be\ninvented. Scope is open too: `fact Spec[Y]` for a FOREIGN carrier Y in a\nsecondary entry to X. R3's allow-list is spelled `fact Spec[X]` (about X) while\nthe module section (line 212) treats orphan provisions for foreign carriers as in\nplay. Allowed or refused? Unstated.\n\nB6. R1's KEY AND R4's LEDGER KEY ARE DECLARED ONE MECHANISM AND DISAGREE ON THE\nCORPUS'S COMMONEST SHAPE. R1 refuses two type declarations \"at one address\"; line\n191 says the ledger is \"keyed on (scope, local name)\" and that \"it is one piece\nof machinery for both\". On the §6.3 eponymous constructor `sort Rec { entity\nRec(…) }` (WI-926; load.rs:2830 reuses the sort's symbol) the two disagree:\naddress-keyed makes it `entity X` + `sort X`, which R1's own list names as an\nerror, so the ledger fails the stdlib on day one; (scope,local-name)-keyed passes.\n\nB7. R2 OVERTURNS 001's \"NAME UNIQUENESS\" -- AN IMPLEMENTED PROPOSAL -- WITHOUT\nSAYING SO. 001:139: \"A name may be declared as either a `sort` or a `domain` in\nthe same scope, not both. … Rationale: … Reopening would introduce complexity\n(what can be added? can constructors be added, breaking closed ADT?) without\nsufficient benefit.\" (`domain` is today's `namespace`.) R2 blesses exactly that\npair as the mechanism. 059 cites the rule only as background and never proposes\nan amendment. Mitigating: the rule was never carried into the spec, so the\nconflict is proposal-to-proposal; and R1/R3 do answer 001's two questions. It\nstill needs saying explicitly.\n\n=== SERIOUS — cited sources misrepresented, or the document contradicts itself ===\n\nM1. 058 §3.6 IS *PROPOSED*, NOT DELIVERED. 058's status line: \"defaults (§3.6)\nand per-provision conditions (§3.8) are proposed\". `one_default`,\nDefaultProvider, no-displacement and \"fill silence, never overwrite speech\" are\nall §3.6. 059 states them as in force -- \"one_default REFUSES a second default\",\n\"the orphan case is ALREADY refused\", \"Within one KB, 058 ALREADY decides it\" --\nand R4 clause 2 is \"058 §3.6's no-displacement, in its own words\". That sits\nbadly against 059's own promise that every claim is measured against the loader.\n(§3.1's nameability gate, which clause 1 uses, IS delivered -- that half is fine.)\n\nM2. SPEC §8.7 \"INSTANCE COHERENCE\" CONTRADICTS 059's LOAD-BEARING WHOLE-PROGRAM\nPREMISE, UNACKNOWLEDGED. §8.7:2326: \"Instance selection is SCOPED, NOT GLOBAL …\ndifferent scopes may resolve the same Spec[carrier] to different providers, the\nper-`import` choice noted above\"; :2324 \"A consumer chooses which instantiation\nto use via `import`.\" 059:207: \"there is no boundary for anything here to cross.\"\n058 §8 proposes replacing that paragraph -- but the amendment is unapplied, so\nthe spec still says the opposite. 059 never cites §8.7.\n\nM3. SPEC §8.7:2357 SAYS A NAMESPACE-LEVEL OPERATION \"BACKS NOTHING\". \"Backing\nmust be reachable THROUGH THE CARRIER … A free operation declared at NAMESPACE\nlevel, with the same name and signature and sitting beside the carrier, is none\nof those and BACKS NOTHING … The implementation therefore goes in the carrier's\nsort body.\" R2 claims and measures the opposite for a namespace AT the carrier's\naddress. The reconciliation is 059's own line-36 measurement (different address\n=> not attached), but §8.7's sentence carries no such qualification, and a reader\nof the spec concludes R2 is impossible. Live, not academic: WI-978 records that\nthe backing check never runs for that placement.\n\nM4. A DISCLAIMED MEASUREMENT IS ASSERTED AS FACT FOUR LINES LATER, AND IT IS THE\nREASON FOR R3's HARDEST REFUSAL. Line 152: \"whether a secondary entry's\n`requires` also changes runtime dictionary layout WAS NOT ESTABLISHED\". Line 156:\n\"Adding a requirement to a type one does not own RE-LAYS-OUT ITS DISPATCH for\nevery one of its users, and that is the single thing in this proposal a module\nboundary would certainly forbid.\" It also collides with line 138 (\"it is not\nabout dictionary layout … there is no cost specific to a secondary entry to price\nthere\") and 207 (\"there is none to charge\"), while 211 restores the layout harm.\nFIX DIRECTION: rewrite 156 in the vocabulary the section already chose -- the harm\nis WHO THE DECLARATION BINDS (an obligation added to every existing caller), not\nre-layout -- and let 211 carry the layout claim scoped to separate compilation.\n\nM5. BOTH \"COMPANION\" CITATIONS ARE MISREAD AS R2's SHAPE. 058's canonical\ncompanion is a SEPARATE WITNESS SORT AT ITS OWN ADDRESS (058:217 \"in lib_b …\nsort MoneyByAmount -- glue: a witness beside a foreign carrier\"); 001's deferred\n`companion` block \"desugars to a separate domain with an implicit import\". Both\nare a different address reached by import -- which 059's own line 9 and line 36\nsay is NOT R2's shape (\"attachment is by address, never by import\"). The one\nproperty R2 exists to supply is MEMBERSHIP, which neither has. So the difference\nis not \"the keyword\" (line 5) and 058 does not \"already contemplate this\nmechanism\" (line 199).\n\n=== COMPLETENESS ===\n\nC1. R3's \"EXHAUSTIVE OVER AN ENTRY'S DIRECT CONTENT\" IS FALSE. Of the 19\n_namespace_content productions, 4 fall through: `describe_declaration` (and it is\nNOT inert -- load_describe asserts DescriptionInfo FACTS, which R3's own \"refuse\nevery other fact\" reaches, and it takes a TARGET, so it writes about a\ndeclaration in another entry -- the identical objection R3 raises against a\nforeign-target proof); bodyless `abstract_sort` (`sort Alias = Int64` -- the\nallow-list is qualified \"with a body\", the refused list covers only `= ?`); and\nthe two standalone type-param binders `sort ?T` / `sort [T]`, where the braced\nform `sort [F] { sort [T] }` HAS a body so the allow-list admits what the\nrefused-list means to ban. Separately, R3 classifies a production that cannot\nappear: a bare `{< … >}` description block is a PARSE ERROR in a namespace body.\n\nC2. LINE 199 PRESCRIBES A SPELLING THAT DOES NOT PARSE. `namespace Rec { provides\nShow[T = Rec] }` is a parse error -- `provides_clause` is admitted only by\n_sort_content/_enum_content; a namespace body admits `provides_block` (with a\nmandatory `language L`) and `fact_declaration`. R3's allow-list is accidentally\nright; line 199 contradicts it. And 058 §4's proposed retirement of the `fact`\nspelling would leave a secondary entry with NO provision spelling at all. The two\nforms are not interchangeable anyway: load_provides_clause takes its carrier from\nthe enclosing domain implicitly, where `fact Spec[X]` writes it explicitly.\n\nC3. THE CENSUS INFERENCE USES THE WRONG POPULATION. \"Counting every DECLARATION\nwhose short name already resolves … gives 61 sites … so the unqualified clause is\nunimplementable.\" 38 of the 61 are parameters and type names, which the clause --\nstated over \"a member OPERATION … capture an OPERATION\" -- never reaches, and\nwhose stated exclusion reasons are definitional (\"a binder is not a declaration\"\n-- contradicting the word \"declaration\" in the count itself). The number for that\nargument is 23, every one an override (19 requires/provides + 4 import). The\nfigures themselves are sound: 38 + 19 + 4 + 0 = 61 = 63 - 2, and the pre-WI-982\ntable was the broken one (40 was the enclosing-ROUTE count, not the class count;\n40 + 19 + 4 + 2 = 65 != 63). Independently reproduced: 427 declarations, 427\ndistinct (scope,name), 0 duplicates -- and 0 addresses carrying both a namespace\nand a type, i.e. the corpus contains no secondary entries at all.\n\nC4. THE 427-DECLARATION LEDGER MEASUREMENT DROPS ITS OWN EXCLUSION, AND THE\nEXCLUDED ARM IS ONE R3 ALLOWS. WI-981: \"NOT COUNTED: operations declared inside\n`provides … language … end` blocks reach the KB by another path.\" R3 admits\nexactly those blocks in a secondary entry.\n\nC5. OTHER UNCLASSIFIED / UNSTATED CASES.\n  - A nested `sort T { … }` in a secondary entry where T is one of X's TYPE\n    PARAMETERS. R3's allow-list admits it verbatim; `define` merges same-named\n    declarations in one scope into one symbol, so the type parameter is silently\n    displaced -- IDENTITY, the one thing R3's title forbids, caught by nothing.\n  - R4 clause 3 is keyed on operations, but its motivating measurement is BARE-NAME\n    resolution in an unedited body. A const is a member and is reachable bare\n    (measured, line 37), so a secondary entry's const capturing a bare name is the\n    same silent meaning-change with no clause over it. Same for a nested sort/enum\n    name capturing an imported type name.\n  - R2's CONVERSE is never stated: a provision declared in a secondary entry,\n    BACKED BY A MEMBER OF THE MAIN ENTRY. Line 80's \"a satisfaction fact belongs\n    in the closure where its backing exists\" reads as a per-entry closure, which\n    would refuse it and contradict the one-scope model. WI-978 has to implement\n    the check and is not told which closure.\n  - PROOF TARGETS ARE NOT ONLY RULES. load_proof resolves contract_proof_target_qn\n    first -- a proof may target an operation contract <op>.<clause> (WI-539/025),\n    which has no rule symbol. R3's proof clause is argued entirely from\n    set_proof_result writing back onto the target RULE.\n  - TWO HOST `provides … language rust … end` BLOCKS for one (spec, carrier,\n    language) across a main and a secondary entry. 058's one_default arbitrates\n    PROVIDERS, not host carrier/operation_map realization tables (WI-876's\n    per-carrier host-op keying). Which mapping wins is unstated.\n  - `internal` VISIBILITY IS NEVER MENTIONED. It is real and enforced (WI-369,\n    §8.6). Because attachment is by address and the scope is shared, anyone may\n    write a secondary entry at a foreign sort's address and read every `internal`\n    member of it. That is a fourth item for \"Where this leads\", which names three.\n  - R1's pair-list omits `enum X` + `sort X`, `entity X` + `entity X`, and every\n    pair involving `sort X = T`. The general clause covers them; the colon reads\n    as the definition.\n\nC6. SMALLER, EACH MECHANICAL.\n  - Line 152/156 aside, the \"one question\" count is three in three places (205,\n    209, 217) and FOUR in a fourth (215). The classification flip was appended as\n    a tail clause to bullet 3 (commit 8df4fb93) and only 215 was recounted.\n  - R5 (line 201) covers only OPERATIONS; line 154 cites it for \"reaching\n    REQUIREMENTS too\", which R5 does not say.\n  - Line 181 asserts all three R4 clauses are stated \"over the scope\"; clause 2 is\n    stated over secondary entries in its own text, and \"the scope\" read literally\n    includes an ordinary namespace, which line 56 promises R3/R4 never reach. Say\n    \"over the sort's scope -- main entry and secondary entries alike\".\n  - Line 188 concedes clause 2 is \"a sub-case of 1\" and never says what it refuses\n    that 1 does not. (It is the asymmetric main-vs-secondary case, justified by\n    no-displacement rather than nameability -- say that, or fold it in.)\n  - Line 81 \"Refused, each naming the sort:\" has no blank line before it, so it\n    renders INSIDE the last allowed bullet -- the allowed list's last entry heads\n    the refused list.\n  - Line 86 promises \"the two conditions below\", 26 lines away; line 87 then says\n    \"the same condition\" singular, meaning condition (2).\n  - Line 123 reads as a self-contradiction (\"undecidable\" then \"decidable at scan\n    time\" two sentences later); it is right, but needs \"once both close\".\n  - Line 39 \"the three questions this proposal was asked\" -- never stated in the\n    document; they are WI-975's.\n  - Line 16 undersells its own finding: the `entity X` + `sort X { … }` fixture is\n    REFUSED (\"unresolved name 'Int64' in scope\"), not \"loads\".\n  - R6's placement splits R3 in half (R3's requires-material runs 138-156, after\n    R6 at 127-136), and R6 is forward-cited at 105 and 123 anyway, so the stated\n    reason for the position does not hold. Reading order is R1,R2,R3,R6,R4,R5.\n  - Line 103 \"a a secondary entry\"; line 136 \"§WI-896\" (a § on a ticket id); line\n    144's costs table has an empty header row.\n  - Status line: \"Every claim below is measured against the Rust loader\" is\n    contradicted by the document at 87 and 152 and by the whole prescriptive R6\n    and module sections. Scope it to the measurement tables.\n  - Spec §11 `NamespaceContent` (kernel-language.md:2692) is itself stale w.r.t.\n    proposals 039 and 025/031 -- it omits `const` and `proof`, which R3\n    classifies. Reconcile before \"anything unlisted is refused\" becomes code.\n\n=== FIXED IN PLACE (factual errors, no design judgment) ===\n\nF1. \"the sort's OWN member already outranks other supply routes at dispatch\n(WI-842 refuses a tie …)\" -- self-contradictory and false. WI-842's rule is \"LOUD\nON THE SECOND CANDIDATE, never first-match\"; it DELETED the carrier_own_op\nranking. Rewritten: a member does not outrank, a tie is refused, and that is the\nstandard R4 restores.\n\nF2. The WI-346 hand-off over-claimed. Driven with a both-sides control against\ncheck_requires_shadows (load.rs:6412-6446): fires on requires+own-op, absent with\n`fact Show[T=Rec]` added, absent with no requires. The derived conclusion (the\ntwo never co-fire) holds, but WI-346 covers only the requires-WITHOUT-provides\nsubset. Narrowed, and a note added that this clause's exclusion is wider than the\nspec's `override` (§8.7:2363 says a requires-user's same-named op \"is NOT\noverriding it: that operation is unrelated\"), so the clause needs its own term.\n\nF3. The builtin-tag caveat had ROTTED INSIDE ITS OWN COMMIT: it cited the two\nWI-982 sites as the example, and WI-982 declared them, so a reader checking\n`nonvar` today finds a declaration and concludes the paragraph is wrong. Measured\nover all 54 registered tags: 51 declared, 3 not -- `anthill.kernel.find_dictionary`\n(kinds [\"Operation\"], in no .anthill file) is now the named example.\n`anthill.reflect.typing.is_entity_of` is declared only as a RULE that joins the\ntag-minted symbol. And `anthill.reflect.Expr.ho_apply` rides an ENTITY symbol, so\n\"an operation by kind\" is not even universally true -- a check keyed on\n`kind == Operation` misses it. Both corrections are in the text.\n\n=== VERIFIED, NO ACTION ===\n\nAll five 058 verbatim quotes exist exactly once and mean what 059 uses them for\n(except M1's status and M5's companion). §6.3's \"operations move a free-standing\nentity to the long form\", §\"A rule head functor is resolved, not declared\",\n§\"A rule-introduced functor is scoped where it is written\", and the `[simp]`\ntyper-rewrite claim are all verbatim-accurate. No cited work-item status is\nmisstated. Dot dispatch requires membership with no free-function fallback --\ndriven both ways (`f(Rec(n:7))` => 7; `Rec(n:7).f()` => refused\n\"no such member (dot dispatch)\", typing.rs:384). A const is a member and not on\nthe dispatch surface -- driven both ways. The six measured symmetry directions\nmatch the six table rows line 90 counts. WI-857's dictionary-bundling claim is\nits delivered settlement. line 18's \"pass 1 gates the parent link on the\ndeclaration being new\" is verbatim at load.rs:2711.\n\nOne citation is misattributed rather than wrong: §6.7 is \"Field Access (Dot\nProjection)\" and says nothing about method dot dispatch or a free-function\nfallback. The claim is supported by the other half of the citation, WI-279.", at: "2026-08-04T11:27:15Z")
